Subject: [PATCH] layout: fall back to fonts of other scripts for missing codepoints

skb__itemize() split a script run by font, but chose only from the fonts
that support the run's script. Digits and punctuation (script Common) take
the script of the text next to them, so in "<shalom> 123" with the fonts
[Noto Sans Hebrew, Noto Sans], in either order, the whole text was one
Hebrew run in Noto Sans Hebrew, which has no digits: they got glyph 0,
though Noto Sans, which has them, was in the collection.

- When none of the fonts of the script has a codepoint, the codepoint's
  grapheme cluster comes from the first font of the family, in the order
  the fonts were added, that has every codepoint of the cluster, as
  browsers do; if no font has the whole cluster, from the first font that
  has the codepoint. The new skb_font_collection_match_fallback_fonts()
  lists the candidates: the same style matching as
  skb_font_collection_match_fonts(), with the codepoint in place of the
  script as the hard constraint (skb__match_fonts() takes the codepoint,
  -1 for the script).
- A fallback font is used only for what the fonts of the script lack:
  the next codepoint they have goes back to them, and each codepoint they
  lack is looked up again, so it comes from the first font that has it.
- Default ignorable codepoints (ZWJ, variation selectors and the rest of
  HarfBuzz's set) never fall back: HarfBuzz hides them, and moving one to
  another font would split the run around it.
- A text whose codepoints the script's fonts all have is itemized as
  before, so its layout does not change.

The fallback run keeps the script run's script, direction and bidi level,
so shaping, bidi reordering and cluster mapping are as for any other font
split; the line takes the fallback font's ascender, descender and line
height into its maxima, as it does for any run.

Found by psy's pack tool, which refuses a glyph 0 (docs/layout.md, "Font
fallback"). Applies to skribidi dee63d6 after skribidi-0001-bidi-run-merge.patch
and skribidi-0002-l1-trailing-whitespace.patch.

diff --git a/include/skribidi/skb_font_collection.h b/include/skribidi/skb_font_collection.h
index 6702814..5be3321 100644
--- a/include/skribidi/skb_font_collection.h
+++ b/include/skribidi/skb_font_collection.h
@@ -354,6 +354,27 @@
 	skb_font_handle_t* results, int32_t results_cap);
 
 /**
+ * Returns fonts matching specific font properties that have a glyph for the codepoint, in the order they were added, regardless of script.
+ * Used for font fallback, when none of the fonts that support a run's script has a codepoint, e.g. digits and punctuation, which take the script of the text around them.
+ * Codepoint and font family are hard constraints, for the rest, we do best effort to find something compatible, as in skb_font_collection_match_fonts().
+ * The font fallback callback is not called.
+ * @param font_collection font collection to use.
+ * @param codepoint codepoint the fonts must have.
+ * @param font_family font family to match.
+ * @param weight weight to match.
+ * @param style style to match.
+ * @param stretch stretch to match.
+ * @param results pointer to results array.
+ * @param results_cap results array capacity.
+ * @return number of fonts matching the parameters.
+ */
+SKB_API int32_t skb_font_collection_match_fallback_fonts(
+	const skb_font_collection_t* font_collection,
+	uint32_t codepoint, uint8_t font_family,
+	skb_weight_t weight, skb_style_t style, skb_stretch_t stretch,
+	skb_font_handle_t* results, int32_t results_cap);
+
+/**
  * Retruns true if the specified font supports given codepoint.
  * @param font_collection font collection to use.
  * @param font_handle font to query.
